    "conclusion": "The available evidence does not support meaningful instability in semantic class under nominally identical questions: exact repeats yielded zero semantic instability across all 16 anchors. It does support limited variability in broader observable response state. Prompt paraphrasing and prior-answer context causally increased full-state distance relative to exact repetition, affecting claim formulation, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referents, and abstention/request behavior while producing no semantic flips. Thus, altered wording and conversational history should be retained as response-state-changing conditions, whereas explanations based on inherent semantic instability under exact repetition should be rejected. Claims about finer-grained changes should be qualified by measurement reliability: semantic-class and action findings are strong, claim-state findings are less secure, and abstention, referent, and exact evidence-set conclusions warrant caution."
